## What happened

.NET 11 removed the built-in DSA implementation on macOS. Apple's native cryptography framework (CryptoKit / Security.framework) has never supported DSA, and Microsoft previously shimmed it via a managed/OpenSSL fallback. In .NET 11 that fallback was dropped, so `DSA.Create()` now throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS.

## How to fix it

**Option A – Bring your own OpenSSL (short-term, keeps DSA)**

Install OpenSSL via Homebrew and use `DSAOpenSsl` explicitly:

```csharp
using System.Security.Cryptography;

DSA dsa;
if (OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    // Requires the System.Security.Cryptography.OpenSsl package
    // and OpenSSL installed (e.g. brew install openssl)
    dsa = new DSAOpenSsl();
}
else
{
    dsa = DSA.Create();
}
```

Add the package reference:

```xml
<PackageReference Include="System.Security.Cryptography.OpenSsl" Version="11.0.0" />
```

**Option B – Migrate away from DSA (recommended long-term)**

DSA is a legacy algorithm (FIPS 186-2, max 1024-bit keys in most implementations). If you only need to **verify** old signatures as a one-time migration gate, do so, then move to ECDSA or Ed25519 for anything new:

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create(ECCurve.NamedCurves.nistP256);
```

## Summary

| | .NET 10 | .NET 11 |
|---|---|---|
| macOS DSA | Worked (managed shim) | `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| Linux/Windows DSA | Works (OpenSSL / CNG) | Still works |

**Recommendation:** Migrate to ECDSA/Ed25519. If you must keep verifying legacy DSA signatures on macOS, explicitly use the OpenSSL-based provider with a runtime platform check.
